@* The guide to the Glyph Regex page. *@
<p class="lead">
    Every glyph is matched with a regular expression the engine builds from its class: its literal words, its vocabularies'
    members, and a named group for each property. Glyph Regex shows those expressions, one card per glyph, with each
    named group in its own color. It is the page to open when the question is
    <strong>"what exactly does this glyph match, and how often?"</strong>
</p>

<h4>A card</h4>
<p>
    The header names the glyph and counts its <strong>matches</strong>: how many times it captured text anywhere in the corpus,
    at the top level or nested inside another glyph. The body is the expression, formatted so a named group opens on its
    own line and its contents sit indented beneath it; a group's color is the color its captures take on the other pages.
    A long expression is cut short, with a <em>show all</em> link; the copy button in the corner copies it whole.
</p>
<p>
    Hover a group's name and the same group lights up everywhere in the card - its opening, its contents and its closing -
    while the rest fades, which is how a deeply nested expression is read one group at a time.
</p>

<h4>Formats</h4>
<p>The <span class="icon oi oi-code"></span>toggle on the top row cycles through five ways of writing a vocabulary's members, from fullest to tersest:</p>
<table class="reference">
    <tr><th>Full</th><td>Every member, alphabetically, each with its count in the corpus - including the ones that never matched.</td></tr>
    <tr><th>Matched enums only</th><td>Only the members that occurred, most frequent first. The default.</td></tr>
    <tr><th>Pruned</th><td>As above, and any group that never captured anything - an alternative never taken, an optional part never present - is left out, with everything inside it. A glyph that never matched is pruned whole.</td></tr>
    <tr><th>Sample</th><td>At most three members per vocabulary, the most frequent, one row each.</td></tr>
    <tr><th>Minified</th><td>The compiled expression as the engine runs it, in one piece, still colored by group.</td></tr>
</table>
<p>The <span class="icon oi oi-tag"></span>toggle chooses how groups are named: by the <em>shortest unique</em> ending of their full name, or <em>numbered</em> by their position in the expression (<code>_17_IsNegated</code>), which stays unique however deep the group sits.</p>

<h4>The footer tray</h4>
<p>The <span class="icon oi oi-layers"></span>toggle opens a tray under each expression:</p>
<table class="reference">
    <tr><th>Matches</th><td>Every capture of the glyph in the corpus: the document, and the captured text colored by group.</td></tr>
    <tr><th>Type tree</th><td>The glyph's groups as a collapsible tree - the shape of the expression without its text.</td></tr>
    <tr><th>C# class</th><td>The glyph's declaration, as written, colored by group, with breadcrumbs into the glyphs its properties name.</td></tr>
</table>

<h4>Other controls</h4>
<table class="reference">
    <tr><th>Search</th><td>Keeps the glyphs whose name contains the term.</td></tr>
    <tr><th><span class="icon oi oi-bar-chart"></span>Order by occurrence count</th><td>Most-matched glyphs first; off, alphabetical.</td></tr>
    <tr><th><span class="icon oi oi-ban"></span>Hide zero-capture types</th><td>Leaves out glyphs that never matched - which, in a grammar at full coverage, is the list of glyphs that could be deleted.</td></tr>
    <tr><th><span class="icon oi oi-minus"></span>Hide blank lines</th><td>Closes up the spacing between groups.</td></tr>
    <tr><th><span class="icon oi oi-list"></span>Show line numbers</th><td>Numbers the formatted lines, for talking about one.</td></tr>
</table>

<div class="callout tip">
    <span class="callout-label">What to look for</span>
    <p>A vocabulary with hundreds of members and three matched is a vocabulary doing little work. An expression that runs to thousands of lines in <em>Full</em> and a dozen in <em>Pruned</em> is a glyph far wider than the corpus asks it to be.</p>
</div>
